  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n\nThe central idea is to explore representations based on **Fourier series** combined with **piecewise linear waves** due to their ability to generate functions with well-distributed discontinuities. This approach directly connects to the **discrete periodic signal processing nature** of the objective function. Function spaces with Fourier basis have been shown to represent functions minimizing certain extremal correlation metrics, and this strategy is distinct from the parent's gradient-only optimization as it leverages specific harmonic structures.\n\nScientific justification stems from:\n\n- Some known extremal problems in analysis (Bergstr\u00f6m's inequality) show optimality of sine waves in some correlation minimization problems\n- For constrained minimization, the set of periodic functions provides a compelling mathematical class\n- Fourier-form representation offers efficient parameterization even in higher dimensions\n- Contrasted with local optimization, global search of Fourier modes explores meaningful mathematical structure rather than optimization landscape\n\nImportant limitations:\n- Rigorous proof of benefit in this specific case is not established\n- Flat-bottomed pulses might miss optimal solutions\n- Time factor restricts potential modes to 12 or fewer\n\nSolver selection rationale:\n- The projected Newton method (L-BFGS-B) is mathematically well-justified\n- Smoothed log-entropies provide convex relaxations for maximum projection constraint\n- The Fourier transform naturally integrates with autocorrelation metrics\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n\n**Representation:**\n- Mode decomposition: h(x) = mean + \u2211_k a_k \u03c8_k(x)\n- Where \u03c8_k are selected periodic basis functions\n- Primary basis choices: sin(2\u03c0kn/2), cos(2\u03c0kn/2), sawtooth(ramp function)\n- The function space is defined on [0,2] with periodic boundary (oscillator mode)\n\n**Variables:**\n- Amplitude coefficients a_k for k=1 to M modes\n- Offset parameter c (bias)\n- Gain parameter g (overall magnitude)\n\n**Objective Function (Continuous variables):**\nMinimize C = max_{t} \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+t)) dx over all shifts t\n\n**Discrete Objective:** equivalent to max-k correlation of (h and 1-h)\n\n**Feasibility Constraints:**\n- 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 \u2200x \u2192 parameterized to enforce bounds\n- \u222b_0^2 h(x) dx = 1 \u2192 explicit enforcement via conditional penalty\n\n**Surrogate Objective Formulation (for optimization):**\nMinimize f(a,g,c) = min(1e6, max_k [smooth_approx(c_k)]) + \u03bc||(\u2202h/\u2202x)||\u2082\u00b2\nwhere smooth_approx(c_k) = (c_k)^(1-\u03b5) + \u03b5*log(1 + sum(exp(c_k - warmup)))\n\n**Implementation Summary:**\n1. Build hierarchical basis matrices for Fourier, sin, cos, and sawtooth modes\n2. Project parent solution to nearest valid Fourier expansion\n3. Use sequential quadratic programming to minimize maximum correlation\n4. Column generation adds modes adaptively (frequency and position)\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n\n**Step 1/7: Initialization Blocks**\n- Single pulse: h(x) = rect((x - \u03c6)/w) or similar windowed functions\n- Double well: two equal humps separated by d\n- Triangulate wave forms (valid basis functions)\n\n**Step 2/7: Basis Parameterization**\n```python\ndef build_basis(n):\n    basis = np.eye(n)\n    # Fourier modes\n    for k in range(1, min(6, n//4+1)):\n        basis = np.vstack([basis, np.sin(2*np.pi*k*np.arange(n)/n),\n                            np.cos(2*np.pi*k*np.arange(n)/n)])\n    # Sawtooth\n    basis = np.vstack([basis, np.r_[np.min(basis)**2, np.arange(1, n)+np.linalg.norm(basis)**2]])\n    return basis.T\n```\n\n**Step 3/7: Objective Computation**\n- Precompute all shifts for given h via fast convolution\n- Track current max correlation value\n- Use multiple random shifts for stochastic approximation\n\n**Step 4/7: Mode Expansion Strategy**\n1. Start with single basis\n2. Add worst-performing basis component\n3. Reoptimize coefficient vector\n4. Update active set \u2192 \u0393-expansion\n\n**Step 5/7: Projection Functions**\n```python\ndef project_feasible(h, n, tol=1e-3):\n    diff = np.sum(h) * (2.0/n) - 1.0\n    if abs(diff) < tol:\n        return h\n    for _ in range(2000):\n        if abs(diff) < tol:\n            return h\n        if diff > 0:\n            room = np.clip(1 - h, 0, 1)\n            idx = np.random.choice(len(room))\n            h[idx] = np.clip(h[idx] + diff * room[idx], 0, 1)\n        else:\n            total = np.clip(h, 0, 1).sum() if not hasattr(h, 'dtype') else np.sum(h)\n            mass = np.clip(h, 0, total)\n            idx = np.random.choice(len(mass))\n            h[idx] = np.clip(h[idx] + diff * mass[idx], 0, total)\n    return np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n```\n\n**Step 6/7: Hierarchical Optimization**\n1. Train at n=40\n2. Upsample to n=100 if time > threshold\n3. Final optimization at requested resolution\n(2) Mode pruning for memory conservation\n(3) Multiple initial condition runs\n\n**Step 7/7: Timer Management**\n```python\ndeadline = time.time() + budget_s\ncheck_time = deadline - 30  # Final check\nif time.time() > deadline - 60:  # Warning\n    # Eldorado protocol: reevaluate best so far\n```\n\n4. Search Choices and Useful Variations\n\n**Default Mode Selection:**\n- Harmonic number: 1 to 12 (MCP 2 or 3)\n- Basis type: Fourier (default), sawtooth, half-sine\n- Balancement between model complexity and statistical level \u2248 7\n\n**Hyperparameter Tuning:**\n- Initial amplitude range: [0.1, 0.5]\n- Momentum factor for warmup: 0.95\n- Penalty parameter \u03bc: 1e-2 to 1e-4\n\n**Escaping Local Minima:**\n- Frequency doubling: k_k * 2\n- Model swapping: sinusoidal \u2192 step function\n- Learning rate reset: increase 10x\n\n**Candidate Evaluation Strategy:**\n- Compute objective only for top 5%/1% improvement\n- Stochastic gradient approximation: evaluate 30 shifts for n=100\n- Break-even tolerance: half of current improvement rate\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n\n**Time Estimation:**\n- Baseline run: 600 seconds\n- Upsampling: +400 seconds\n- Mode expansion: +600 minutes\n(Recalculate based on CPU profile)\n\n**Deadline Management:**\n```python\ndef check_timeout(deadline, current):\n    if time.time() > current:\n        if best_candidate is not None:\n            return best_candidate\n        # Fallback to reliable seed/variable combination\n```\n\n**Failure Detection:**\n- Solve stuck detection: >500 evaluations but no improvement\n- Objective divergence: increase from previous during optimization\n- Negative correlation scores (abort)\n\n**Memory Allocation:**\n- Fourier basis: O(n\u00b2) for dense Hessian approximation\n- Stochastic objective: store temp samples\n- Gradient memory: 4 * n (shared with initial condition)\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n\n**Feasibility Check:**\n```python\ndef validate_candidate(h, n):\n    if not np.all((0 <= h) & (h <= 1)):\n        return False\n    # Edge case handling for numerical instability\n    if np.isnan(sum(correlation(h, n) * dx)): return False\n    return True\n\ndx = 2.0 / n\n```\n\n**Objective Computation:**\n```python\ndef compute_c5(h, n):\n    all_modes = np.vstack([np.roll(h, i) for i in range(n)])\n    convolve_result = np.dot(all_modes, 1 - h)\n    return np.max(convolve_result) * dx\n```\n\n**Final Output Guarantee:**\n- h array meeting 0\u2264h\u22641\n- c5_bound value \u2264 parent generation\n- n_points matching intended resolution\n- backup solution (fallback)",
